          %p
            We now discuss four equivalent definitions of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                via hom-isomorphism (Def.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdjointFunctorsInTermsOfNaturalBijectionOfHomSets"}
                below);
            %li
              %p
                via
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction+unit"} adjunction unit
                and -
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction+counit"} counit
                satisfying
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangle+identities"} triangle identities
                (Prop.
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdjointnessInTermsOfHomIsomorphismEquivalentToAdjunctionInCat"}
            %li
              %p
                via
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representing+objects"} representing objects
                (Prop.
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdjointFunctorFromObjectwiseRepresentingObject"}
            %li
              %p
                via
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+morphisms"} universal morphisms
                (Prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CollectionOfUniversalArrowsEquivalentToAdjointFunctor"}
                below).
          %p Then we discuss some key properties:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                uniqueness of adjoints (Prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UniquenessOfAdjoints"}
                below),
            %li
              %p
                epi/mono/iso-characterization of adjunction (co-)units (Prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FullyFaithfulAndInvertibleAdjoints"}
                below).
