          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h3#Modalities Modalities
          %p
            Generally, a
            = succeed "-inclusion" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FullyFaithfulFunctor"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            in a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %p
            This basic situation becomes particularly interesting when the inclusion functor has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            (Def.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdjointFunctorsInTermsOfNaturalBijectionOfHomSets"}
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategory"} coreflective subcategory
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ReflectiveSubcategory"}
            below). The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            now implies that each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflector"} reflected
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflector"} coreflected
            into the subcategory, and equipped with a comparison morphism to or from its (co-)reflection (the adjunction (co-)unit, Def.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdjunctionUnitFromHomIsomorphism"}
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ModalOpIdempotent"}
            below.
          %p
            This means that, while any object may not fully enjoy the property that defines the subcategory, one may ask for the “aspect” of it that does, which is what is (co-)projected out. Regarding objects only via these aspects of them hence means to regard them only
            %em locally
            (where they exhibit that aspect) or only in the
            %em mode
            of focus on this aspect. Therefore one also calls the (co-)reflection operation into the given subcategory a
            %em
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colocalization"}> co
              \-)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comodal+operator"}> co-
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operator"} modal operator
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ModalOperator"}
            below).
          %p
            One finds that (co-)modalities are a fully equivalent perspective on the (co-)reflective subcategories of their fully
            %em
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comodal+object"}> co-
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+objects"} modal objects
            (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ModalObjects"}
            below), this is the statement of Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ModalOperatorsEquivalentToReflectiveSubcategories"}
            below.
          %p
            Another alternative perspective on this situation is given by the concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization+of+categories"} localization of categories
            (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalizationOfACategory"}
            below), which is about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universally
            forcing a given collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"}> weak equivalences
            ”, Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryWithWeakEquivalences"}
            below) to become
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+morphism"} invertible
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+localization"} reflective localization
            is equivalently a
            = succeed "-inclusion" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ReflectiveSubcategoriesAreLocalizations"}
            below), and this exhibits the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+objects"} modal objects
            (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ModalObjects"}
            below) as equivalently forming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+objects"} local objects
            (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalObjects"}
            below).
          %p
            Conversely, every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflection
            onto
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategories"} full subcategories
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+objects"} local objects
            (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalizationAtACollectionOfMorphisms"}
            below) satisfies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with respect to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            (Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ReflectionOntoLocalObjectsIsLocalizationWithRespectToLeftAdjoints"}
            below).
          %p
            In conclusion, we have the following three equivalent perspectives on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
